description:
Write lesson 6.03's data-driven wave spawner:

```cpp
struct Spawn { double time; std::string kind; };

class Waves
{
public:
    bool load(const std::string& text, std::string& error);
    int count() const;                                              // number of waves
    std::vector<Spawn> schedule(int wave) const;                    // every spawn of a wave (1-based), sorted by time
    std::vector<std::string> spawnsBetween(int wave, double from, double to) const;   // spawns with from < time <= to
    double length(int wave) const;                                  // time of the wave's last spawn, 0 if none
};
```

**The format**: lines starting with `#` and empty lines are ignored. `wave N` starts wave N, and waves must be numbered `1, 2, 3...` in order. Every other line inside a wave is a group: `time kind count interval` (time and interval are decimal numbers, count a whole number of at least 1, interval at least 0). A group spawns `count` enemies of `kind` at `time`, `time + interval`, `time + 2 × interval`, ...

Errors (`load` returns false and leaves the previous waves in place): `"line N: expected wave 2"` (the wrong number), `"line N: group outside a wave"`, `"line N: bad group"` (N counting all lines from 1).

`schedule` sorts by time; spawns at the same time keep the order of their groups in the file, then their order in the group. An unknown wave number gives no spawns.

